"The System sells custom teleports to anyone, civilians included, at great cost."
"The Anesidora island government pays for teleports on official travel."
"A traveler gets a notice from the System before a teleport."
"A teleport usually begins with a System phone call, then a countdown."
"It brings a sudden nausea that passes as suddenly as it came."
"A notice may come a minute ahead, naming the destination with a YES/NO prompt."
"An island resident can arrange a teleport for a visitor."
"Arrivals land in phonebooth-sized bays and are asked to submit to a scan."
"Refusing the scan means an in-person evaluation, usually a half-hour wait."
"Anesidora's teleports run on time slots, with a departures lounge and security scanners."
"Cross-dimensional teleports bring no nausea and are not instant; the mind floats bodiless."
"Teleportation alcoves open on their own and zap items away within seconds."
"Local teleports on stable worlds with good equipment are safe even for infants."
"Rough teleports need the traveler's own power to keep them whole."
"Wear from rough teleports builds up more on lower-rank Avowed such as B-ranks."
"Hazardous materials need permits and good reasons to go by System teleport."
"An emergency teleport orders the Avowed to the nearest alcove or to brace, with a countdown."
"A teleportation cycle is started by the sending world's Contract and met by the receiving one's."
"If the far Contract fails, the teleport can fail, and the Avowed is listed missing."
"Non-System teleportation exists but is rare, perhaps only for the truly elite."
"Runed vaults and alcove runes vaporize demons that touch them."
"Summonariums are built large for big or many passengers, and large ones are easier to power."
"The Contract normally sends an Avowed off the Mother Planet soon after arrival."
"Anesidora's arrival bays monitor vital signs; long Triplanet stays can trigger extra screening."
"Artonans are not known for dropping unstable people back on Earth."
